<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch9-page1-autonomous-equations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Autonomous Differential Equations and Equilibria</title>

  <p>
    In this chapter, we cover the parts of Chapter 9 of the book that are
    relevant to what we will be doing in the next chapter. You're welcome to
    look at Chapter 9, but it goes into much more than we cover here, and
    more than we will need!
  </p>
  <p>
    In Chapter 10, we will model populations whose makeup changes over time.
    To do that, we need <term>differential equations</term>, which are
    equations that relate an unknown function to its derivative. In this
    class, we will only look at one kind, called autonomous differential
    equations.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-autonomous-ode">
    <title>Autonomous Differential Equation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An <term>autonomous differential equation</term> is a differential
        equation of the form
        <me>
          \frac{dp}{dt} = f(p),
        </me>
        where <m>p = p(t)</m> is an unknown function of time <m>t</m>, and
        the right side <m>f(p)</m> depends only on <m>p</m>, not on
        <m>t</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    The book often writes <m>\dot{p}</m> for <m>\frac{dp}{dt}</m>, so the
    equation can also be written <m>\dot{p} = f(p)</m>. We will use both.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the simplest example. Suppose a population grows at a rate
    proportional to its size, with proportionality constant 1. If <m>p(t)</m>
    is the size of the population at time <m>t</m>, this says
    <me>
      \frac{dp}{dt} = p.
    </me>
    That is, a solution to this equation is a function <m>p(t)</m> whose
    derivative is equal to itself!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ode-derivative-itself">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which famous function is equal to its own derivative?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = e^t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right! <m>\frac{d}{dt}e^t = e^t</m>, so <m>p(t) = e^t</m> is a
            solution of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = t^2</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The derivative of <m>t^2</m> is <m>2t</m>, which is
            not <m>t^2</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = \ln t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The derivative of <m>\ln t</m> is <m>\frac{1}{t}</m>,
            which is not <m>\ln t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = \sin t</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The derivative of <m>\sin t</m> is <m>\cos t</m>,
            which is not <m>\sin t</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    In fact, <m>p(t) = Ce^t</m> works for <em>any</em> constant <m>C</m>,
    since
    <me>
      \frac{d}{dt}\left(Ce^t\right) = Ce^t.
    </me>
    Plugging in <m>t = 0</m> gives <m>p(0) = Ce^0 = C</m>, so the constant
    <m>C</m> is just the starting value of the population. Each choice of
    <m>p(0)</m> gives a different solution:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ode-exp-solutions">
    <caption>
      Solutions <m>p(t) = Ce^t</m> of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p</m> for several
      starting values <m>p(0) = C</m>.
    </caption>
    <image width="70%">
      <shortdescription>Graph of solutions p of t equals C e to the t for five starting values</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          Five solution curves are drawn for t greater than or equal to 0, starting at
          <m>p(0) = 2</m>, <m>1</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>-1</m>, and <m>-2</m>. The
          curves starting at 2 and 1 rise faster and faster, the curves
          starting at <m>-1</m> and <m>-2</m> fall faster and faster, and the
          curve starting at 0 stays at 0.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
          axis lines=middle,
          xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$p$},
          xmin=-0.05, xmax=1.6, ymin=-6, ymax=6,
          ytick=\empty, xtick={1},
          domain=0:1.6, samples=60,
          clip=false,
          every axis plot/.append style={thick},
          xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
        ]
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:1.0986] {2*exp(x)};
        \addplot[blue!65] {1*exp(x)};
        \addplot[black] {0};
        \addplot[red!65] {-1*exp(x)};
        \addplot[red, domain=0:1.0986] {-2*exp(x)};
        \fill[blue] (axis cs:0,2) circle (1.8pt);
        \node[anchor=east, text=blue] at (axis cs:0,2) {$p(0) = 2$};
        \fill[blue!65] (axis cs:0,1) circle (1.8pt);
        \node[anchor=east, text=blue!65] at (axis cs:0,1) {$p(0) = 1$};
        \fill[black] (axis cs:0,0) circle (1.8pt);
        \node[anchor=east, text=black] at (axis cs:0,0) {$p(0) = 0$};
        \fill[red!65] (axis cs:0,-1) circle (1.8pt);
        \node[anchor=east, text=red!65] at (axis cs:0,-1) {$p(0) = -1$};
        \fill[red] (axis cs:0,-2) circle (1.8pt);
        \node[anchor=east, text=red] at (axis cs:0,-2) {$p(0) = -2$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Mathematically, <m>p</m> can be any real number, including negative
    numbers. For a population, of course, only <m>p \geq 0</m> makes sense.
    (In Chapter 10, <m>p</m> will be a fraction of a population, so only
    <m>0 \leq p \leq 1</m> will matter.)
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-general-particular-solution">
    <title>General and Particular Solutions</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>general solution</term> of a differential equation is a
        formula that describes all of its solutions, usually involving an
        arbitrary constant such as <m>C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <term>particular solution</term> is one specific solution. It is
        usually determined by a starting value <m>p(0)</m>, called an
        <term>initial condition</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    For example, <m>p(t) = Ce^t</m> is the general solution of
    <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p</m>, and <m>p(t) = 2e^t</m> is the particular
    solution with <m>p(0) = 2</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now suppose the population grows at a rate proportional to its size,
    with proportionality constant 3:
    <me>
      \frac{dp}{dt} = 3p.
    </me>
    A quick check shows that anything of the form <m>p(t) = Ce^{3t}</m> is a
    solution, since <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left(Ce^{3t}\right) = 3Ce^{3t}</m>. It
    turns out that these are all of the solutions, so <m>p(t) = Ce^{3t}</m>
    is the general solution. (The same is true for <m>Ce^t</m> above. We
    won't prove that there are no other solutions.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Notice that in both examples, setting <m>C = 0</m> gives
    <m>p(t) = 0</m>, a constant solution. If the population starts at
    <m>p(0) = 0</m>, it stays there forever! We will see that constant
    solutions like this turn out to be very important for understanding the
    behavior of all the other solutions. They are called equilibrium
    solutions.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-equilibrium-solution">
    <title>Equilibrium Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A solution of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)</m> of the form
        <m>p(t) = k</m> for some constant <m>k</m> is called an
        <term>equilibrium solution</term>. We often just call the number
        <m>k</m> an <term>equilibrium</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    To find the equilibria, we don't have to solve the differential equation
    at all. The following fact will be very useful for us!
  </p>

  <fact xml:id="fact-equilibria-zeros">
    <title>Finding Equilibria</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>p(t) = k</m> is an equilibrium solution of
        <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)</m> if and only if <m>f(k) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <title>Justification</title>
      <p>
        If <m>p(t) = k</m> is a solution, then
        <m>f(k) = \frac{dp}{dt} = 0</m>, since <m>p</m> is constant.
        Conversely, if <m>f(k) = 0</m>, then <m>p(t) = k</m> has
        <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = 0 = f(k)</m>, so it is a solution.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </fact>
  <p>
    In general, solving differential equations can be much more complicated
    than it was for something like <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = 3p</m>. Much of what we
    will be doing involves studying the behavior of solutions without
    actually solving the differential equation. In the example below, we find
    the equilibria using the fact above, instead of finding all the solutions
    and picking out the constant ones.
  </p>
  <example xml:id="eg-ode-equilibria">
    <title>Finding equilibria</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equilibrium solutions of
        <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Here <m>f(p) = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m>. Setting <m>f(p) = 0</m> gives
        <m>p = 2</m> or <m>p = -3</m>, so the equilibrium solutions are
        <m>p(t) = 2</m> and <m>p(t) = -3</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ode-equilibria">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which of the following are equilibrium solutions of
        <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p^2 - 1</m>? Don't forget to factor first!
        <alert>Select all that apply.</alert>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = 1</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Yes. <m>p^2 - 1 = (p - 1)(p + 1)</m>, and <m>p = 1</m> makes the
            first factor 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = 0</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            No. <m>f(0) = 0^2 - 1 = -1</m>, which is not 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = -1</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Yes. <m>p^2 - 1 = (p - 1)(p + 1)</m>, and <m>p = -1</m> makes the
            second factor 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>p(t) = 2</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            No. <m>f(2) = 2^2 - 1 = 3</m>, which is not 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-9-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=9.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
